"""Authored data for the Course Review study guides: one argument paragraph
per unit and the key answers to each band's three Carry Three questions.
Everything else in the guides (EQs, terms, definitions, timelines) is pulled
from planner-data.js, the audited staging JSON, and the band packets."""

UNIT_ARGUMENTS = {
"ca": "Three empires and many Native nations met in the Americas, and the Columbian Exchange remade all of them: crops, animals, and diseases crossed the oceans and rearranged ecologies and populations on three continents. The English colonies that lasted were built on mixed motives, joint-stock profit, religious refuge, and land, and they grew into distinct societies: the Chesapeake on tobacco and, increasingly, enslaved labor; New England on family farms, towns, and covenants; the middle colonies on pluralism and grain. Slavery, present from 1619, was written into hereditary racial law across the seventeenth century. Mercantilism bound the colonies to Britain's economy while salutary neglect let their assemblies learn self-government, and the Great Awakening and the Enlightenment supplied two languages, one of conversion and one of reason, for questioning authority.",
"fd": "The imperial settlement after 1763 ended salutary neglect, and each revenue measure met a colonial answer that broadened the argument, from taxation to Parliament's supremacy to the king himself. Independence was declared in the language of natural rights and consent, and won by endurance, French alliance, and British exhaustion. The peace exposed the Confederation's weaknesses, and the founding generation argued the frame out loud: a Constitution balancing energy against fear of power, compromised on representation, on slavery's protection, and on the trade that fed it, ratified only with the promise of a Bill of Rights. The unit's question is what the founding settled and what it deliberately postponed, because the postponed items govern the rest of the course.",
"er": "The new republic had to become a working government. Hamilton's financial system, funded debt, a national bank, and excises, created federal credit and, in the fight over it, the first parties. Washington's precedents and the Neutrality Proclamation kept the republic out of Europe's wars while the Marshall Court built national supremacy into law. The Louisiana Purchase doubled the national domain in the name of strict construction's opposite, the War of 1812 ended in a stalemate its country read as vindication, and the Era of Good Feelings' one-party calm cracked on Missouri in 1820. Underneath the politics, the market revolution, canals, mills, and national roads, was rewiring the economy the Constitution had to govern.",
"jr": "Democracy widened and hardened at the same time. Property tests fell and mass parties mobilized a white male electorate into the modern campaign, while Jackson's presidency fought the Bank as privilege's engine, answered nullification with union, and carried out Indian removal, the era's defining crime, against the southeastern nations. Reformers read the same democratic faith outward: abolitionists demanding immediatism, women at Seneca Falls claiming the Declaration's pronouns, temperance and common-school crusades rebuilding society by statute and schoolhouse. The unit's verdict is that Jacksonian democracy was real for those inside its boundaries and ruthless at them, and that the era's reform movements are the boundaries being tested from within.",
"sc": "For a generation the republic survived slavery by compromising over its expansion, and each settlement bought time at a higher price: 1820's line, 1850's package with its fugitive slave law that radicalized the North it was meant to quiet. Kansas-Nebraska repealed the old line in 1854 and the territories bled; Dred Scott told the country the question could not be compromised at all; a sectional party won the presidency in 1860 on stopping expansion, and the lower South seceded to protect the institution outright. The war that followed, total in scale and revolutionary in result, destroyed slavery, redefined the Union as a nation, and restated its purpose at Gettysburg, at a cost of some 620,000 dead or more.",
"rc": "Reconstruction wrote the war's results into the Constitution: the Thirteenth Amendment abolishing slavery, the Fourteenth making citizens of all born on the soil and promising equal protection, the Fifteenth forbidding race as the franchise's stated ground. In the South, biracial governments built public schools and rewrote state constitutions while freedpeople built churches, schools, and families the slave regime had outlawed. The settlement's limits were as structural as its promises: no land was redistributed, the Supreme Court narrowed the amendments within a decade, paramilitary terror raised the price of enforcement, and the North's depression-era fatigue cashed out in the bargain of 1877. What Reconstruction settled, citizenship in law, and what it left open, citizenship in fact, frame the next hundred years.",
"ga": "Industrial capitalism reorganized American life in a generation: the corporation and the national market, railroads binding a continent, steel, oil, and electricity remaking production, and cities doubling on immigration's new sources. The gains and the costs were distributed by the same machine. Labor organized to answer, the Knights' broad crusade and the AFL's craft discipline, and met the injunction, the blacklist, and the troops; farmers organized into the Alliance and then Populism, the era's most serious third-party challenge. At the bottom of the order, the South wrote Jim Crow into law and disfranchisement into constitutions, Plessy blessed the fiction of separate equality, and lynching enforced what statutes could not say.",
"pe": "Progressivism was the country's organized answer to the Gilded Age's dislocations, run through three channels at once: the democracy repaired (primaries, initiatives, the direct election of senators, and finally the Nineteenth Amendment), the corporation regulated (railroad rates, antitrust revived, pure food and meat laws, the Federal Reserve, conservation of the public lands), and the city and workplace made survivable (settlement houses, labor law, the muckrakers' exposure that powered all of it). Its presidents, Roosevelt, Taft, and Wilson, disagreed on means and shared the premise that the federal government was now the economy's referee. The movement's standing failure was the color line, which most of its white leadership accepted and its federal government, under Wilson, extended.",
"wwi": "At the century's turn the United States took an empire and argued about it in the open: markets, naval strategy, and mission carried the flag to the Philippines and Puerto Rico, and the anti-imperialists answered in the Declaration's own terms. The Caribbean became an American lake policed by the Roosevelt Corollary, and the Panama Canal made the two-ocean navy possible. In 1914 neutrality was policy and profit at once; by 1917 unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann telegram had closed the argument. Mobilization built the modern war state and policed its home front, the Espionage and Sedition Acts, Schenck's clear and present danger, and the Fourteen Points won the war's diplomacy and lost the peace's in the Senate.",
"tw": "The Twenties' prosperity was real and narrow. Mass production and consumer credit remade daily life, the automobile, radio, and advertising building a national culture, while the era's politics argued over the culture itself: Prohibition's experiment and its repeal movement, the revived Klan, immigration restriction written into law, Scopes, and the Harlem Renaissance's counter-statement. The weak spots stood in plain sight: agriculture depressed all decade, income concentrating while wages lagged production, credit and margin speculation inflating the market the crash then repriced. The unit's verdict is that 1929 revealed the prosperity's structure more than it caused the Depression that followed, which is why the next unit begins in the spiral, not the crash.",
"dp": "The Depression was deflation's machine: falling prices making debts heavier, bank failures destroying credit and savings together, a quarter of the labor force idle by 1933. Hoover answered actively and stopped at direct relief; Roosevelt crossed that line in his first week and experimented for eight years, the Hundred Days' alphabet, the Second New Deal's structures, Social Security, the Wagner Act, the WPA, until the coalition he built held national politics for a generation. The unit's ledger is double: the modern safety net, labor's legal standing, insured deposits, and a federal government answerable for the economy's health on one side; full employment unachieved, the era's exclusions written into its programs, and the 1937 recession proving how much of the recovery was the spending itself, on the other.",
"ww2": "The war did what the New Deal had not, by spending at ten times the scale and drafting the surplus into uniform: the arsenal of democracy made the United States the Grand Alliance's supplier, a two-ocean belligerent whose production decided both wars, on an Eastern Front where the Soviet Union broke the German army and across a Pacific taken island by island to the atomic end. At home the war state rationed, priced, and propagandized; it opened factory gates to women and, under pressure, to Black workers behind the Double V's demand, and it imprisoned 120,000 Japanese Americans on ancestry, a judgment the Supreme Court sustained. The war's reckonings, the Holocaust exposed, Nuremberg's new law of individual responsibility, and the GI Bill's returning generation, are the next era's starting conditions.",
"cw": "Containment began as a premise, that Soviet expansion would crack under patient pressure, and acquired a house by 1950: the Truman Doctrine's pledge, the Marshall Plan's billions, NATO's alliance, and NSC-68's global military posture. Asia tested it first, China read as a loss, Korea fixing limited war as the American way of fighting one. At home the same fear ran the Second Red Scare's machinery until television broke McCarthy, while the nuclear shadow deepened from monopoly to hydrogen brinkmanship to deterrence's mutual hostage. Cuba taught both capitals the game's price; Vietnam applied the doctrine where its premises fit worst; détente managed the rivalry; and the end came from inside, when Gorbachev's reforms released everything they were meant to save.",
"cr": "The movement stood on decades of construction, the NAACP's litigation ladder, the Double V generation's claims, Truman's committee and desegregation order, the Black church's networks, before Brown declared segregation's law void and massive resistance answered. Its engine was nonviolent direct action: disciplined protesters manufacturing a crisis in a place chosen for its opponent's reliability, national television carrying it into the federal government's in tray, at Montgomery, Greensboro, Birmingham, and Selma. The legal harvest, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, remade the South's public order in five years. What the statutes could not reach, housing, wealth, the northern economy, is the unfinished agenda the movement itself named at Memphis and the Kerner Commission documented.",
"ma": "Modern America opens with the postwar settlement's collapse, stagflation and the tax revolt, and the conservative turn that replaced it: lower taxes, loosened regulation, labor broken at PATCO, deficits accepted as the price. The Cold War's end was cashed as globalization, production integrated across borders, the Rust Belt emptying into the service economy, while the digital transformation rebuilt commerce, news, and politics in sequence. The population remade alongside, the 1965 immigration act's long fuse, the Sun Belt's rise, cities sorted by flight and return. Then the emergencies set the terms: September 11's security state and open-ended authorization, Iraq's failed intelligence case, the Great Recession's bank-first rescue, and populisms on both flanks, all inside the polarization the unit closes on.",
}

BAND_KEY_ANSWERS = {
1: ["Chesapeake: tobacco worked first by indentured servants and then by enslaved Africans, the labor made hereditary and racial by statute; belonging was a hierarchy of Englishness in which freedom and land belonged to white men of property. New England: family farms and town crafts worked by free household labor; belonging was the covenanted community, membership granted by church and town and policed by both. The middle colonies: a mixture, family farms, indentured servants, enslaved people in smaller numbers, and wage labor in the ports; belonging was pluralist by charter and commerce, several faiths and peoples trading under one toleration.",
    "1763's Proclamation and the end of salutary neglect; the Sugar Act (1764), answered by the constitutional argument against taxation without representation; the Stamp Act (1765), answered by a congress of colonies, boycotts, and crowds, broadening the claim to all internal taxes; the Townshend duties (1767), answered by nonimportation, broadening it to Parliament's supremacy itself; the Tea Act (1773), answered by the destruction of the tea, turning argument into action against property; the Intolerable Acts (1774), answered by a Continental Congress and militia, making the resistance continental and armed before Lexington.",
    "Representation: the Connecticut Compromise gave the small states an equal Senate and the large states a proportional House, and each side carried home a chamber it could defend. Slavery and power: the Three-Fifths Compromise counted enslaved people at three-fifths for representation and direct taxes, so the South carried home amplified power in the House and the Electoral College, and the North carried home the admission that representation would follow that count. The trade: importation of enslaved people was protected until 1808, so the South carried home twenty years, and the trade's opponents carried home a date on which Congress could act."],
2: ["The Missouri Compromise (1820) settled the Senate's balance for a generation, Maine against Missouri, and drew the 36°30' line across the Louisiana Purchase; it postponed the underlying question, whether slavery could expand at all and what it was, by ruling only where. The Monroe Doctrine (1823) settled the hemisphere's diplomatic posture, European colonization closed and American abstention from Europe's wars pledged; it postponed enforcement, which rested for decades on British sea power and only later on American.",
    "In 1798 the theory was Jefferson's and Madison's: the Virginia and Kentucky Resolutions answered the Alien and Sedition Acts with interposition, and the theory stayed on paper, answered in the end by the election of 1800. In 1832 it was Calhoun's and South Carolina's: a state convention's ordinance nullifying the tariff, answered by Jackson's proclamation denying that any state could nullify a federal law or leave the Union, and by the Force Bill beside a compromise tariff. Its fate: as constitutional law it died with the Confederacy, the war settling what resolutions could not; as rhetoric, the claim that a state may judge federal power, it has never left American politics.",
    "Widened: property qualifications for voting fell until white manhood suffrage was the national norm, and mass parties, nominating conventions, and popular campaigning raised turnout and made the president the people's tribune in form. Hardened: Indian removal expelled the southeastern nations from the polity's map altogether, by treaty fraud and by the Army's roads west; and Black citizenship contracted in the same years, Pennsylvania striking Black voters from its rolls in 1838, New York keeping a property test for Black voters alone, the gag rule tabling abolitionist petitions in the House."],
3: ["The Compromise of 1850's package, whose Fugitive Slave Act radicalized the North it was meant to quiet, was broken by Kansas-Nebraska (1854), which repealed the Missouri line and made the territories a shooting war's prize. The settlement of two national parties was broken by the Republican Party's rise on slavery's nonextension and the Democracy's split in 1860. The hope of a judicial settlement was broken by Dred Scott (1857), which denied Black citizenship and Congress's power to bar slavery from the territories. The electoral settlement was broken in 1860, when a sectional victory the rules fully allowed was answered as illegitimate. The Union itself was the last settlement broken, by secession in the winter of 1860 and 1861.",
    "The Thirteenth settled slavery's abolition in every state, forever, in the Constitution's text; it left open what freedom would mean in land, labor, and protection. The Fourteenth settled citizenship, all persons born on the soil, and armed the Constitution against the states with due process and equal protection; it left open enforcement, pointedly wrote the word male into the Constitution on the franchise question, and gave the South's representation a penalty it never collected. The Fifteenth settled that race could not be the franchise's stated ground; it left open every unstated ground, the tests, taxes, and terror the next century administered.",
    "From the courts: Slaughterhouse (1873) emptied the Fourteenth Amendment's privileges or immunities, Cruikshank (1876) disarmed federal protection against private violence, and the Civil Rights Cases (1883) voided the public-accommodations act, so the law's teeth were drawn in the decade of its writing. From the economy: the Panic of 1873 and the long depression turned northern capital, attention, and congressional majorities toward their own distress, and the South's experiment lost its funder and its audience. From violence and politics: the Klan, the White Leagues, and rifle clubs raised enforcement's price in blood at Colfax and Hamburg and a hundred smaller places, while Democratic redemption and northern fatigue lowered the will to pay it, and the bargain of 1877 cashed both ledgers."],
4: ["The Knights of Labor organized broadly, all producers in one movement, skilled and unskilled, Black and white, women and men, and aimed past wages at the wage system itself, toward a cooperative commonwealth. The AFL under Gompers organized narrowly and durably, craft by craft, for wages, hours, and conditions inside capitalism, pure and simple unionism. The legal weapon that defeated strikes most reliably was the injunction: a federal court order against a strike or boycott, issued on equity grounds, enforced by contempt proceedings without a jury and by troops when needed, with Pullman in 1894 the exhibit and the Sherman Act, written against trusts, applied to the union as a combination in restraint of trade.",
    "Democracy repaired: the Australian secret ballot adopted state by state, the direct primary, initiative, referendum, and recall, and the Seventeenth Amendment's direct election of senators. The corporation regulated: the Interstate Commerce Commission given rate power by the Hepburn Act, the Sherman Act revived in Northern Securities and extended by the Clayton Act, the Federal Reserve System of 1913, and the Pure Food and Drug and Meat Inspection Acts of 1906. The electorate widened: the state suffrage campaigns and then the Nineteenth Amendment (1920), and, in the same channel, the era's school and franchise reforms that made the electorate a mass public the parties had to address in print.",
    "The Gilded Age built the color line as a legal system: southern states disfranchised Black voters by constitution and statute through the 1890s, segregation was codified across public life, Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) supplied the fiction of separate but equal, and lynching enforced what the statutes left unsaid, thousands murdered with near-total impunity. Progressivism, with honorable exceptions, did its work on the line's white side and often deepened the system: the Wilson administration segregated the federal civil service in 1913, southern progressives made white-only primaries and at-large schemes into reforms, and the national movement priced the South's Black citizens out of its coalition."],
5: ["Markets and materials: an industrial economy outproducing its home demand needed outlets and inputs abroad, and the depression of the 1890s made the need feel urgent. Sea power: Mahan's argument that national greatness ran on a great navy, coaling stations, and an isthmian canal, which made distant bases a strategic necessity rather than a luxury. Mission: the claim, racial in its terms, that Anglo-Saxon peoples were fitted and obliged to carry their civilization and government to peoples deemed unready for it. The anti-imperialists' answer, in their own platform's logic, was one sentence long: a republic founded on the consent of the governed cannot rule subjects abroad without becoming the empire it was born against.",
    "Structure first: from 1914 the United States traded and lent overwhelmingly with the Allies, built a preparedness program in 1915 and 1916, and organized a National Defense establishment, so neutrality's economics and machinery leaned one way while its law leaned straight. Incidents second: the Lusitania (1915) and the crises after it were managed by diplomacy into pledges, the Arabic and Sussex pledges narrowing Germany's submarine practice without war. The closers came in early 1917: Germany's announcement of unrestricted submarine warfare from February 1, a decision to accept American belligerency as the price of starving Britain, and the Zimmermann telegram's publication in March, proposing Mexico an alliance against the United States, which made neutrality untenable in public as the sinkings were making it untenable in policy.",
    "Agriculture had been depressed all decade: overproduction against shrinking export markets, collapsing prices, and farm debt carried at wartime values. Incomes were concentrated while wages lagged productivity, so mass purchasing power could not buy what mass production could make, and installment credit papered the gap. The market itself was leveraged, stocks bought on margin against borrowed money, with the Florida land boom's collapse in 1926 as the rehearsal nobody heeded. The crash revealed rather than caused these because the demand failure was already built: when credit and confidence vanished in October 1929, what stood exposed was an economy whose production had outrun its customers' incomes for years."],
6: ["Falling prices made every debt heavier in real terms, so farmers and firms defaulted as their incomes shrank; the defaults and the runs together killed the banks, some nine thousand of them, and with the banks went the savings and the credit of whole regions; firms that were sound on any normal accounting failed for want of loans, cut production and payrolls, and a quarter of the labor force stood idle, collapsing demand and pulling prices down again. Hoover stopped at indirect action: the Reconstruction Finance Corporation lending to banks and railroads, public works, pressure on employers and charities, and no direct federal relief and no abandonment of the gold standard and the balanced budget. Roosevelt began by crossing the line in his first week, the bank holiday and federal inspection of every reopened bank, and within the Hundred Days came federal relief itself.",
    "Scale: the war state spent at multiples of the New Deal's peak and sustained the spending until the economy ran past full capacity, so the demand the New Deal had rationed by politics was supplied without limit. The draft: conscription pulled some eleven million workers directly out of the labor market into uniform, so war spending did not bid against a surplus, it met empty benches, and unemployment fell under two percent.",
    "Victory over fascism abroad and victory over racism at home, the Pittsburgh Courier's pairing. Abroad, advanced by the cause's own stated terms, the Four Freedoms, and by Black service in every theater; betrayed abroad by the segregated army that did the fighting and the blood policy that separated plasma by race. At home, advanced by Executive Order 8802 and the FEPC, won by Randolph's threatened march, and by the defense jobs themselves; betrayed at home by internment, 120,000 people imprisoned on ancestry in the same years, and by the zoot suit attacks on the communities of Mexican American servicemen."],
7: ["Containment's premise, Kennan's, was that Soviet power, expansionist from insecurity and ideology alike, bore the seeds of its own decay and would crack or mellow under firm, patient counter-pressure at shifting points, applied politically and economically before militarily. By 1950 it was housed in three instruments: money, the Marshall Plan's recovery billions that rebuilt Western Europe against despair's politics; alliance, NATO (1949), the first peacetime alliance in American history, garrisoning the doctrine; and military posture, NSC-68's call for a permanent global buildup, which the Korean War's outbreak let Washington fund at triple the defense budget.",
    "It manufactured crisis: disciplined, trained protesters violated an unjust local law openly, accepted the assault and the arrest without answering either, and did it where the local opponent, a Bull Connor, could be relied on to supply the brutality on schedule. Television carried it: the national networks and the wire photographs moved the crisis out of the town and into the country's living rooms and, decisively, into the federal government's in tray, converting a city ordinance into a national emergency within a news cycle. It forced the federal executive's hand, repeatedly and by design: Eisenhower's at Little Rock, Kennedy's at Birmingham, Johnson's at Selma, each intervention the movement's strategy collecting its debt.",
    "The Cold War ended from inside the Soviet system: a reformer, Gorbachev, opened it to save it, glasnost letting the grievances speak and perestroika loosening the command economy without replacing it, until Eastern Europe's peoples walked out in 1989 and the Union dissolved itself in December 1991, the war containment's planners had armed against for forty-five years never fought. The movement's unfinished agenda, when its legal war was won in 1964, 1965, and 1968, was the structure underneath the statutes: segregated housing and the wealth homeownership builds, joblessness and an urban economy abandoning its workers, schools sorted by the address the housing market assigned, the agenda King had carried to Memphis and the Kerner Commission named as two societies forming."],
8: ["Taxes: the 1981 act cut the top marginal rate from 70 percent to 50, and the 1986 reform brought it to 28, making lower marginal rates and a broader, flatter code the default settlement both parties governed inside for a generation. Regulation: the era's posture treated rules as drag, continuing deregulation across administrations and loosening finance's in particular, a line that runs to the Glass-Steagall repeal of 1999. Labor: the PATCO firing of 1981 announced from the presidency itself that a strike against the settlement would be broken from the top; union density declined through the era, and the strike as labor's central weapon fell out of American practice.",
    "Any two threads, run in three stops, earn the points; the model here runs America in the world and federal power. America in the world: the Farewell's restraint (1796), trade with all and alliance with none, gives way to 1898 and 1917, empire and intervention debated and taken, and then to 1945's settlement, a permanent architecture of alliances, bases, and guaranteed trade, inside which the AUMF's open-ended war (2001) is the present tense. Federal power: Philadelphia's frame, strong enough to act and limited enough to trust, becomes the New Deal settlement, the federal government as the economy's referee and the citizen's insurer, and arrives at the modern doubling, a regulatory and security state of New Deal scale beside a tax politics and a Court committed to suspicion of it.",
    "The attacks of September 11, 2001 broke trust in the government's competence to protect: the security establishment had the hijackers' fragments and missed the plot, as the 9/11 Commission's record established, and the Iraq case that followed, stockpiles that were not there, broke trust in its candor on the same account. The crisis of 2008 broke trust in candor and fairness together: a financial system the government had deregulated and then rescued, at public expense and with no account rendered by those who ran it, while the households whose mortgages were the crisis's fuel were left to the foreclosure process."],
}
